DAY 25 - PART A: THEORY
PersistentVolumes, PersistentVolumeClaims, StorageClasses
Time: 40 minutes
==================================================================

1. THE THREE OBJECTS
--------------------
PersistentVolume (PV)        a piece of real storage in the cluster
                             (cloud disk, NFS share, local disk).
                             Cluster-scoped. Created by an admin or
                             dynamically.
PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)  a REQUEST for storage by a user:
                             "I need 5Gi, ReadWriteOnce, class fast".
                             Namespaced. Pods mount PVCs.
StorageClass (SC)            a "type" of storage + the provisioner
                             that creates PVs on demand.

Analogy: PV = a parking space, PVC = your parking ticket, StorageClass
= the parking company that builds a new space when you buy a ticket.


2. STATIC vs DYNAMIC PROVISIONING
---------------------------------
Static:   admin creates PVs in advance; PVCs bind to a matching one.
Dynamic:  PVC names a StorageClass; its provisioner (a CSI driver)
          creates the disk + PV automatically. This is the norm.

  Pod -> PVC -> (StorageClass -> CSI driver creates disk) -> PV -> disk


3. ACCESS MODES
---------------
  ReadWriteOnce (RWO)      mounted read-write by ONE NODE (block disks:
                           EBS, Azure Disk, PD)
  ReadOnlyMany (ROX)       many nodes read-only
  ReadWriteMany (RWX)      many nodes read-write (file storage: NFS,
                           EFS, Azure Files, Filestore)
  ReadWriteOncePod (RWOP)  exactly one POD


4. RECLAIM POLICY (what happens to the disk when the PVC is deleted)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  Delete   (default for dynamic) disk deleted too  <- careful!
  Retain   PV and disk kept; admin cleans up manually


5. VOLUME BINDING MODE
----------------------
  Immediate             create the disk as soon as the PVC exists
  WaitForFirstConsumer  wait until a pod uses it, then create the disk
                        in the SAME ZONE as the node the pod is
                        scheduled on (important in the cloud, Week 7)

PVC status: Pending -> Bound.


6. A PVC AND A POD
------------------
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
  metadata:
    name: data
  spec:
    accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
    storageClassName: standard     # omit = default class
    resources:
      requests:
        storage: 1Gi
  ---
  spec:
    volumes:
    - name: data
      persistentVolumeClaim:
        claimName: data


7. CSI: THE STORAGE PLUGIN INTERFACE
------------------------------------
The Container Storage Interface lets any vendor write a driver:
  AWS EBS CSI, Azure Disk CSI, GCE PD CSI, Ceph, NetApp, Longhorn,
  local-path (kind's default).
CSI drivers also do snapshots (VolumeSnapshot) and expansion
(allowVolumeExpansion: true -> edit PVC size upward).


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Pod -> PVC -> PV -> real disk; StorageClass creates PVs on demand.
- RWO = one node; RWX needs file storage.
- reclaimPolicy Delete removes the disk; use Retain for important data.
- WaitForFirstConsumer avoids zone mismatches.
